# PART B — TITLE TAGS, META DESCRIPTIONS & HEADINGS

### 6. Optimize the title tag of every indexable page

Every indexable page must have a unique, accurate, descriptive HTML `<title>`.

For each title:

- Lead with the clearest service or search-intent phrase when appropriate.
- Include Dubai or Karama where it helps communicate relevant local intent.
- Include ESS, KBC, or the correct customer-facing business name when useful.
- Communicate the actual service offered.
- Convey convenience, assistance, or trust only when supported by the page and business facts.
- Avoid keyword stuffing, repetitive location names, exaggerated claims, and unnecessary boilerplate.
- Avoid duplicate titles across service pages.
- Keep titles concise and meaningful, then review how they appear in real search results.

Possible title pattern:

`[Service Name] in Dubai | [Accurate Benefit] | [Business Name]`

This is a template, not a requirement to force all three elements into every title. Select the wording that best matches the individual page and its search intent.

Do not put “official government service” or imply that the business is a government authority unless that statement is legally accurate and substantiated.

### 7. Optimize meta descriptions for relevance and clicks

Every important indexable page should have a unique, useful meta description.

The description should normally communicate:

1. The exact service or problem being addressed.
2. The relevant location, when appropriate.
3. The genuine convenience or assistance offered.
4. A meaningful trust signal supported by facts.
5. A clear, natural next step.

Example structure:

“Need [service] in Dubai? [Business name] helps customers with [accurate scope of assistance]. Check requirements and fees, then contact our team to get started.”

Rewrite this example for the actual service. Do not publish generic descriptions across all pages.

Make the title, meta description, H1, visible page content, and target search intent **consistent in meaning**, not mechanically identical. The title should identify the page, while the description should persuade the right user to click.

Google may generate a different title link or snippet based on the search query and page content. Do not promise that Google will always display the supplied description.

Do not use the meta keywords tag as an SEO tactic.

### 8. Audit H1, H2, H3 and semantic headings

For every page:

- Confirm that the main service topic is clearly represented in the visible H1.
- Keep the H1 specific to the page rather than repeating the sitewide slogan.
- Use H2s for major user questions and content sections.
- Use H3s for subordinate topics where needed.
- Ensure headings accurately describe the content that follows.
- Remove empty, duplicated, irrelevant, or decorative headings that create confusion.
- Do not use headings solely to insert more keyword variations.
- Check that headings are actual semantic HTML elements, not merely styled `<div>` elements.
- Ensure the mobile layout preserves readability and heading hierarchy.

Create a page-by-page report of title, description, H1, H2 coverage, duplicates, missing fields, and recommended fixes.

### 9. Improve visible page content

Check that each service page clearly explains, where applicable:

- What the service is
- Who the service is for
- What ESS/KBC actually does
- Which authority or organization administers the underlying process
- Eligibility and limitations
