# PART C — TECHNICAL SEO & CRAWLABILITY

### 10. Audit URL architecture

Inspect every route and ensure that each important service page has a stable, unique, shareable URL.

Preferred principles:

- Use descriptive, readable URLs.
- Use lowercase slugs and consistent hyphenation.
- Avoid unnecessary parameters and duplicate URL variants.
- Ensure each page can load directly when its URL is entered into the browser.
- Ensure refreshing a service page does not produce a 404.
- Ensure important pages are reachable through normal crawlable HTML links.
- Use HTTP 200 for valid pages.
- Return correct 404 or 410 responses for genuinely unavailable content where appropriate.
- Avoid creating multiple indexable URLs for the same page.
- If the website is a JavaScript application, verify that Google can discover and render each unique page.
- Evaluate server-side rendering or prerendering if it materially improves crawl reliability, page performance, or initial HTML availability.

Do not rely on hash fragments such as `#service/example` as the only way to identify different indexable service pages. Use real URL paths for separate pages where feasible.

Do not change existing URLs casually. If a route migration is required, prepare a redirect and internal-link migration plan first.

### 11. Canonical URLs

Audit canonical tags on every indexable page.

Requirements:

- Each unique, indexable service page should normally have a self-referencing canonical pointing to its preferred absolute URL.
- Canonicals must use the correct protocol, hostname, path, and trailing-slash convention.
- Canonicals must not point every service page to the homepage.
- Canonicals must not point to redirected, broken, irrelevant, or non-indexable URLs.
- Ensure the canonical agrees with internal links and the XML sitemap.
- Resolve HTTP/HTTPS, www/non-www, trailing-slash, capitalization, and parameter-based duplicates consistently.
- For genuinely duplicate or near-duplicate URLs, choose the preferred URL and use redirects where appropriate.
- Inspect canonicals in the rendered HTML as well as the original response where applicable.
- Do not use canonical tags as a substitute for correct redirects or appropriate `noindex` controls.

For English and Arabic pages, make sure language alternatives are not incorrectly canonicalized to the opposite language. Establish the correct canonical and hreflang relationship for each language version.

### 12. Robots.txt and indexing controls

Check:

- `robots.txt` exists at the correct root URL.
- It does not accidentally block important pages, CSS, JavaScript, images, or required resources.
- It does not unintentionally block the service directories or Arabic pages.
- The sitemap declaration is correct if included.
- No important page has an unintended `noindex` directive.
- No important page is blocked from crawling while relying on Google to read its `noindex` tag.
- Staging or development environments are not accidentally indexable.
- Production pages do not retain development robots directives.
- Internal search results, tracking parameters, private areas, and low-value utility URLs are handled appropriately.

Do not block URLs indiscriminately. Inspect the current implementation and the actual purpose of each route before changing directives.

### 13. XML sitemap

Create or verify a valid, production-accessible XML sitemap.

Requirements:

- Include only canonical, indexable, successful URLs that should appear in search results.
- Include all important English and Arabic pages.
- Exclude redirected URLs, 404 pages, `noindex` pages, and non-canonical duplicates.
- Use absolute URLs with the correct production hostname.
- Use valid XML syntax and the correct namespace.
- Use `lastmod` only when a meaningful content modification date is available; do not update it falsely every day.
- Implement a sitemap index if multiple sitemaps are useful for the site size or architecture.
- Keep the sitemap synchronized when pages are added, removed, or redirected.
- Ensure the sitemap does not contain development URLs or staging domains.
- Submit the sitemap in Google Search Console after deployment.

If the site has language alternatives, implement hreflang annotations correctly in the pages or sitemap. Avoid redundant or contradictory implementations.

Validate the final sitemap and report the number of submitted URLs, valid URLs, excluded URLs, and any mismatches. Sitemap submission does not guarantee indexing.

### 14. HTTP status codes and redirect audit

Test:

- All primary navigation links
- Every service page
- All breadcrumb links
- All CTA destinations
- Language-switcher links
- Internal links in the footer
- XML sitemap URLs
- Old URLs that may already have been published

Identify 404 errors, 5xx errors, redirect chains, redirect loops, broken internal links, incorrect canonical targets, and links to staging environments.

Use permanent redirects for permanent URL changes when appropriate. Preserve relevant traffic and link equity by redirecting old URLs to the closest equivalent replacement, not automatically to the homepage.

Do not redirect unrelated old pages to irrelevant destinations.

### 15. JavaScript SEO and rendering

For every important page, verify that:

- The main content exists in the rendered DOM.
- The title, description, canonical, robots directives, headings, and structured data correspond to the correct URL.
- Important text is not available only after a user clicks a button.
- Navigation links use crawlable anchor elements with meaningful `href` values.
- Critical content does not depend on inaccessible scripts or blocked resources.
- Direct navigation and browser refresh work.
- Client-side navigation does not leave stale metadata from the previous page.
- Googlebot can access the same meaningful content as users.
- No essential content is hidden from crawlers through implementation errors.

Use URL Inspection and rendered-page testing after deployment.

